{
  "validity": "fail",
  "quality": "weak",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "access",
      "location": "design.access_plan / role user_robert_chen / role membership_robert_chen / role negative_cross_workspace_reaction",
      "problem": "The entire test hinges on being able to determine, per-reaction, whether the reactor's workspace-membership role is 'admin' specifically in the SAME workspace that owns the reacted-on channel (binding_note explicitly requires this). This requires querying a user's role for a particular team_id. However, the runtime_qualifications state that 'users.info exposes one selected workspace association' and instruct designers to 'give each queried person one workspace membership' for reproducible profile predicates. The design deliberately violates this by giving Robert Chen two memberships with different roles (member in T01WORKSPACE, admin in T02NEURO) and Robert Walsh potentially compared across workspaces, then relies on the solver distinguishing per-team role via users.info/team membership data. There is no documented API that returns a user's role filtered by an arbitrary team_id; conversations.members returns membership without role, and users.info collapses to one selected association. This means the negative_cross_workspace_reaction case (Robert Chen admin-in-T02 reacting on a T01 message) and the core match/negative discrimination cannot be reliably resolved through the documented, supported API surface — the access path for the decisive predicate is unsupported/ambiguous.",
      "repair": "Either (a) restrict every reactor in this scenario to a single workspace membership (as the runtime qualification demands) and redesign the cross-workspace negative using a genuinely single-membership user who is admin elsewhere and only a member here, verifiable through a supported lookup, or (b) drop the same-workspace-admin binding requirement to something the documented API can actually verify (e.g., admin status resolvable via a single users.info call scoped to the actor's own workspace), or (c) mark this as a capability-limitation case rather than an ordinary route task."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The prompt's binding condition (reactor must be admin in the SAME workspace owning the message) is exactly the interesting part of this route, but the design's own supporting roles (dual-membership users Robert Chen/Robert Walsh across two workspaces, and the negative_cross_workspace_reaction case) require per-team role lookups that the documented API and the stated runtime_qualifications explicitly disallow ('users.info exposes one selected workspace association'; reproducibility requires one membership per queried person). This is a genuine access/capability defect, not merely a mechanical labeling issue: the decisive predicate distinguishing matches from the cross-workspace negative cannot be reliably computed by an actor using only the documented API calls listed in the access_plan. Everything else (route usage, mode=multiple with two genuine matches, negatives for wrong emoji and non-admin reactor, new workspace/channel/message additions being permissible design-time additions) is otherwise reasonable, but the core validity issue above is disqualifying."
}
```

```json
{
  "validity": "fail",
  "quality": "weak",
  "issues": [
    {
      "kind": "validity",
      "location": "design.prompt / design.binding_note / private.selector.focal / private.construction_explanation",
      "problem": "The prompt reads 'a message which received a rocket reaction from a user who is an admin of a workspace.' In ordinary English, when a query is enumerating workspaces by a property of their own channels/messages/reactors, an unqualified 'an admin of a workspace' attached to that reactor is most naturally read as an admin of the workspace under evaluation (the same one whose channel holds the message) — this is exactly the reading the case's earlier sibling version used explicitly ('that same workspace'). This design deliberately strips the anaphoric qualifier and then uses its own private selector/route-contract ('r5 is joined to r4 only, no equality requiring r5.team_id = r0.team_id') to justify treating 'admin of a workspace' as 'admin of ANY workspace, possibly unrelated to the one being evaluated.' That is using the construction to dictate the semantics rather than deriving the construction from a faithful reading of the prompt, which the review standard explicitly disallows ('do not accept an assigned path merely because it occurs in the private selector'; 'Code's query is a construction claim, not authority over the prompt'). Under the natural reading, workspace_neuro should NOT match, because its only rocket reaction (from Morgan Freeman) has no connection to T02NEURO's admin structure — Morgan Freeman is not admin of T02NEURO at all, he is merely an admin of an unrelated workspace (T01WORKSPACE) who happened to react in T02NEURO's channel. This produces an incorrect/contrived positive match.",
      "repair": "Restore the natural, workspace-scoped semantics: require the reactor's workspace-membership admin role to be in the SAME workspace as the channel/message (r5.team_id = r0.team_id), as the prior version of this case correctly did. If the intent is genuinely 'admin anywhere' the prompt must be rewritten to unambiguously say so (e.g., 'from a user who is an admin of any workspace, not necessarily this one'), since the current plain wording will be read by an ordinary user/solver as same-workspace admin."
    },
    {
      "kind": "quality",
      "location": "design.roles / workspace_neuro match rationale",
      "problem": "Because the qualifying relationship for workspace_neuro depends entirely on an admin-status fact that is causally and referentially unrelated to workspace_neuro itself (Morgan Freeman's admin row in T01WORKSPACE), the resulting task is not really testing 'does this workspace have a channel reacted to by its own admin' but an almost arbitrary global fact about the reactor's admin status anywhere, which weakens the meaningfulness of the multiple-match answer set and could confuse a careful solver who applies the natural reading and would exclude workspace_neuro.",
      "repair": "Same as above — enforce same-workspace binding; this also improves quality by making every match a genuine, contextually coherent instance of the described relationship."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The core defect is a semantic-fidelity failure: the prompt's relationship phrase is translated into entity edges that omit the natural equality between the reactor's admin workspace and the workspace being evaluated, and this omission is justified only by appeal to the private selector/route contract rather than by a faithful reading of the English prompt. This is precisely the kind of translation error the review protocol requires catching (distinguishing what the prompt plausibly asks from what the private construction encodes). Because one of the two intended positive matches (workspace_neuro) depends entirely on this unjustified de-scoping, the referent set as constructed does not correspond to the ordinary requested selection, so the case fails validity despite being internally consistent and mechanically well-formed (base seed, bindings, negatives, and access plan are otherwise coherent and the earlier access-defect from the prior revision has been fixed)."
}
```
